# Data-row changes are cached — evict, then verify

A Flyway migration that only inserts or updates rows looks like the cheapest possible change.
It is also the one most likely to appear to do nothing, because the reader in front of the
table is a cache that survives a service restart.

## The rule

After changing **any** row that the platform caches by key, evict the key **before** believing
any test result. A green run against a stale key proves nothing about your change.

| Row changed | Cache | Key | Evict |
|-------------|-------|-----|-------|
| `notification_message` (error/response text) | Redis **NOTIFICATION** (db `2`) | `<tenant>_<code>_<locale>` | `redis-cli -n 2 --scan --pattern '*<code>*'` then `del` |
| `code__notification_code__mapping` | same as above — the resolved message is what is cached | `<tenant>_<code>_<locale>` | same |
| Loan product / scheme / IAD / accounting rule | Redis **ACCOUNTING** (db `5`) + Spring `accountingCacheManager` | see `.cursor/redis-key-registry.md` | `@CacheEvict` path, or key `del` |

Full key inventory: **`.cursor/redis-key-registry.md`**. Redis db map: `RedisDBConfig`
(`NOTIFICATION=2`, `ACCOUNTING=5`).

## Symptom index — reach for this when you see

- "I changed the row and the API still returns the old value"
- "the migration ran but nothing changed"
- "it works on a fresh environment but not on this one"
- QA reports old wording after a build that definitely contains the new text

All four are the same thing: a cache in front of the row. Check the key **before** re-reading
code. Resolution path for messages is
`NotificationUtil.getResponseMessage` → `cacheClient.get(tenant, code + "_" + locale, NOTIFICATION)`
→ falls back to the `getMessage` internal API only on a miss.

## Known gap

**GAP-058** — the notifications message cache is written with `set` and **no TTL**, so a stale
entry persists until something evicts it. That is why this is a standing hazard rather than a
"wait for expiry" inconvenience.

## Why this rule exists

TDPQA-241 reworded one message. The row was updated, accounting was rebuilt and restarted, and
the API still served the old text — a restart does not clear Redis. The fact was already written
down in `redis-key-registry.md` and the consequence in GAP-058, but nothing connected either to
the act of editing a notifications migration, so it was rediscovered by reading platform-lib
line by line. This rule is that connection.
